window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-10-20T04:15:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-10-19","event":"NAHB housing market index; Steel Dynamics, Cleveland-Cliffs, Zions, Western Alliance and W.R. Berkley earnings","why":"Done. NAHB fell to 29 against 31 expected, the lowest since June 2012, with 43% of builders cutting prices; Cleveland-Cliffs beat and rose 16.3%; Steel Dynamics missed after hours; the S&P 500 rose 0.4% and the 10-year rose 6 bp to 5.15%"},{"date":"2026-10-20","event":"Housing starts and building permits, September; GDPNow update; November WTI expiry; GE Aerospace, GM, RTX, Northrop, Halliburton, Netflix, Capital One, Intuitive Surgical and United earnings","why":"Starts consensus 1.30M after 1.275M and permits 1.39M after 1.403M, with NAHB at 29 and mortgage rates above 7%; GDPNow was 3.6% on 15 Oct; Netflix consensus EPS $0.82 after M Science cut its subscriber estimate"},{"date":"2026-10-21","event":"$13B 20-year Treasury auction; EIA crude inventories; Tesla, Meta, IBM, Texas Instruments, Lam Research, Abbott, AT&T, CME Group, NextEra and Thermo Fisher earnings","why":"Prior 20-year stop 5.42% with the 10-year back at 5.15%; Tesla and Meta (consensus EPS $0.45 and $5.94) are the first big-tech AI-capex tests after the chip rout; EIA prior was an 8.6M barrel draw"},{"date":"2026-10-22","event":"Initial jobless claims, week to 17 Oct; Conference Board leading index; H.4.1 balance sheet; Procter and Gamble, Honeywell, Union Pacific, Valero, Blackstone and Western Digital earnings","why":"Claims consensus 200K after 199K, five weeks under 200K; above 230K would confirm the weak payroll print; leading index consensus −0.2%"},{"date":"2026-10-23","event":"UMich sentiment, October final; S&P Global flash PMIs; American Express and SLB earnings","why":"Preliminary 46.3 with consensus unchanged at 46.3, current conditions at a record low of 44.7 and year-ahead inflation expectations at 4.7%; flash manufacturing PMI consensus 55.5 after 55.9, services 58.0 after 58.8"},{"date":"2026-10-27","event":"Durable goods orders, September; Israel's election","why":"Ex-transportation rose 0.3% in August with core capex up 1.6%; Netanyahu says he does not favor a preemptive strike on Iran before the vote, and Trump endorsed him on 18 Oct"},{"date":"2026-10-28","event":"FOMC decision","why":"A hike is about 15% priced per CME on 19 Oct with Kalshi at an 85.5% hold; December is about 89%; Daly and Bowman said there is time, Williams, Collins and Barkin back one more hike this year; Warsh declined to pre-judge"},{"date":"2026-10-29","event":"PCE inflation, September; Q3 GDP advance estimate","why":"Core PCE at 3.0% for six months; GDPNow at 3.6%; September CPI at 3.7% and PPI at 6.3% imply upside risk to headline PCE"},{"date":"2026-11-02","event":"ISM Manufacturing PMI, October","why":"Prior 54.5; new orders 55.3, prices paid 77.9"},{"date":"2026-11-03","event":"US midterm elections","why":"Fiscal path, and the end of Trump's stated pause on striking Iran"},{"date":"2026-11-04","event":"ISM Services PMI, October","why":"Prior 54.9; prices index 74.0"},{"date":"2026-11-05","event":"White House committee hearing on Fed Governor Cook","why":"Cook's lawyer has asked to move it to after the 8-9 Dec FOMC, citing biased committee members; a test of Fed independence"},{"date":"2026-11-06","event":"October jobs report","why":"After the 29K September print; claims have stayed under 200K for five weeks"},{"date":"2026-11-10","event":"October CPI","why":"After 3.7% headline and 2.6% core in September; the last CPI before the 8-9 Dec FOMC, where a hike is priced as near certain"},{"date":"2026-11-16","event":"Fed field hearing on AI and productivity","why":"Warsh called the AI transition likely to be very bumpy; the Beige Book cited AI-related hiring restraint in five districts"},{"date":"2026-11-17","event":"Industrial production, October; retail sales, October","why":"After September's 0.7% IP gain and 0.8% retail sales rise that was mostly gasoline; a control group print below zero would show sentiment reaching spending"}],"conflicts":[{"a":"Surveys, industrial production and GDPNow say strong growth","b":"Payrolls say hiring has nearly stopped, ex-gas retail sales rose only 0.2%, and NAHB builder sentiment is at a 14-year low of 29","read":"Industrial production rose 0.7% in September and is 2.2% above a year ago, both ISM surveys are near 55 and claims are 199K, but the IP gain leaned on utilities up 3.5% while business equipment was flat, payrolls were 29K, and builders report 43% cutting prices with mortgage rates above 7%. The pattern is low hiring, low firing, output up, housing down and spending inflated by fuel.","trigger":"Housing starts and permits on 20 Oct against 1.30M and 1.39M consensus; claims above 230K on 22 Oct would confirm real labor weakness; durable goods on 27 Oct show whether core capex orders held up."},{"a":"Consumer sentiment at 46.3, with current conditions at a record low of 44.7","b":"Retail sales up 0.8% in September and real spending up 0.6% in August","read":"Households are spending by saving less; the saving rate is 4.1%. Gasoline sales rose 6.4% in the month and Brent is near $110, so more of the nominal strength will be price. Ex-autos-and-gas sales rose 0.2%, and the Beige Book says spending is concentrated among high-income households.","trigger":"The October final sentiment reading on 23 Oct, consensus 46.3, and October retail sales in mid-November; a control group print below zero would show the mood reaching real spending."},{"a":"PPI 6.3%, CPI 3.7%, import prices up 8.1% on the year, Brent near $110","b":"A 28 Oct hike priced at about 15% and the 2-year at 4.77%, 11 bp below its end-September close","read":"The market read Warsh's refusal to pre-judge as a hold in October, and although the 10-year rebounded 6 bp to 5.15% on 19 Oct it remains 16 bp under its 5 Oct closing high while the inflation pipeline got hotter on every measure. December is about 89% priced, so the disagreement is about timing rather than direction, but the gap between the data and October pricing is the widest of the cycle.","trigger":"A 28 Oct hike above 50% priced, or a 10-year close back above 5.31%, would mean the market has stopped looking through the data. The 20-year auction on 21 Oct tests long-end demand."},{"a":"Earnings growth above 30%, semiconductors up 1.6% after an eight-day losing streak, the VIX back to 18.44","b":"Equal-weight S&P 500 down 0.3% with decliners beating advancers 1,761 to 945, high-yield spreads at 323 bp near the October high, the KBW bank index down six straight weeks","read":"Monday's bounce was the AI complex and Amazon; the rest of the market fell as yields rose. CoreWeave's Goldman upgrade came with a target cut to $106 from $139 and a stock down 43% since August, and the high-yield series on FRED is posted only to 15 Oct, so credit has not yet marked Friday's selloff. The VIX at 18.44 and the 10-year at 5.15% leave both halves of the volatility trigger unmet.","trigger":"A high-yield spread above 350 bp, or a VIX close above 20 with the 10-year above 5.31%. Netflix on 20 Oct and Tesla, Meta, Texas Instruments and Lam Research on 21 Oct are the first big-tech and chip capex tests."},{"a":"Gulf flows at about 80% of pre-conflict levels, Iraq exporting 3.4M barrels a day, Araghchi offering a day-six reopening, dated Brent down $16 on the week to $117","b":"A wartime weekly high of 10 attacks per UKMTO, 23 since 1 Oct, a supertanker mined, Houthis targeting Yanbu, MOAB strike plans kept on the table for after 3 Nov","read":"Flows have recovered but attacks have not stopped, and the political pause runs to the midterms. The administration is easing the squeeze from the supply side, waiving Russia sanctions for diesel and legitimizing sanctioned tankers, which keeps futures near $110 rather than higher but does not change the risk. Oil near $110 feeds straight into October CPI and PPI.","trigger":"A Brent settlement above $115, or a confirmed US strike on Iranian territory; a durable reopening deal is the other tail. Israel's 27 Oct election is the next political date."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"The S&P 500 rose 31.29 points to 7,690.73, the Nasdaq 290.85 points to 26,430.89, the Russell 2000 0.3% to 2,728.30; the Dow fell 36.38 points to 51,966.03. The Philadelphia semiconductor index rose 1.6% to 11,316.34, ending its longest losing streak since 2010; consumer discretionary rose 1.8% and technology 1.4%, Amazon rose 5.0% on a Morgan Stanley top-pick call, Cleveland-Cliffs 16.3% on a beat. The equal-weight S&P fell 0.3% and NYSE decliners beat advancers 1,761 to 945; utilities fell 2.0%, real estate 1.2%, financials 0.8%, and the KBW bank index has fallen six straight weeks, the longest since 2008. Goldman upgraded CoreWeave to Buy with the target cut to $106 from $139; JPMorgan upgraded Intel to Neutral. The VIX fell 7.8% to 18.44. The 10-year rose 6 bp to 5.15%, the 2-year 3 bp to 4.77% and the 30-year 5 bp to 5.48% as yields rose worldwide after European indexes fell more than 1% on weekend protests. The dollar index rose 0.15% to 101.99; December gold fell 0.5% to $4,160.10. Steel Dynamics fell 5.4% after hours on a miss. Netflix reports 20 Oct; Tesla, Meta, IBM, Texas Instruments and Lam Research on 21 Oct.","src":"AP via myMotherLode; Investrade; Cappnotes; US Treasury","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 rises 0.4% to 7,690.73 and the Nasdaq 1.1% as semiconductors end an eight-day losing streak; 10-year back up to 5.15%","url":"https://mymotherlode.com/news/national/11235328/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-monday-10-19-2026.html"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"The NAHB/Wells Fargo housing market index fell 3 points to 29 against a 31 consensus. Current sales conditions fell to 33 from 35, the six-month sales outlook to 32 from 37 and prospective buyer traffic to 18 from 23. Regionally the Northeast was 38, the Midwest 40, the South 30 and the West 26. 43% of builders cut prices, the highest share since 2020, by an average 6% for a seventh month, and 65% used incentives, a 19th straight month at or above 60%. NAHB said mortgage rates have climbed well above 7%. Housing starts and permits for September print on 20 Oct with consensus of 1.30M and 1.39M.","src":"NAHB; Investrade","tag":"Data","title":"NAHB builder sentiment falls to 29 in October, the lowest since June 2012; 43% of builders cutting prices","url":"https://www.nahb.org/news-and-economics/press-releases/2026/10/builder-sentiment-falls-on-rising-rates"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"December Brent settled 0.3% lower at $109.55; November WTI, expiring 20 Oct, rose 2.2% to $103.66 and December WTI fell 0.5% to $98.54; dated Brent traded near $117, down almost $16 on the week. Energy Secretary Wright said Persian Gulf flows have recovered to about 80% of pre-conflict levels with refined products still constrained, and that sanctioned tankers are being allowed to legitimize cargoes to curb prices; Iraq's oil minister said exports exceed 3.4M barrels a day this month. Iran said it has no choice but to assert control over Hormuz; UKMTO said two tankers reported being hit in the Persian Gulf; Houthis targeted Yanbu and a Saudi helicopter crashed near the Yemen border. Kpler said trend-following funds are at maximum long in Brent and WTI. Sparta expects Brent to stay near $100 because the rest of the economy cannot absorb higher prices. Putin reportedly proposed talks with Trump and Xi next month; a US official said Trump has not agreed.","src":"Bloomberg via Rigzone","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Brent settles at $109.55 near a five-month high as Gulf flows recover to about 80% while attacks continue; expiring WTI jumps 2.2%","url":"https://rigzone.com/news/wire/oil_prices_hover_near_fivemonth_high-19-oct-2026-184878-article"},{"date":"2026-10-18","impact":"Araghchi said Hormuz would reopen on day six and negotiations start on day seven if the US meets Iran's September seven-day conditions, including lifting the blockade on Iranian ports, and that Tehran is still reviewing US comments. UKMTO said at least 10 vessels were attacked in or near Hormuz in the week, the highest since the war began in February, and 23 since 1 Oct, almost double the average; a tug was hit by two projectiles off the UAE. On 17 Oct the IRGC said a supertanker carrying more than 2M barrels hit a mine and caught fire, and a tanker was hit by an RPG off al-Jubayl. Iran's new defense minister Akhlaghi warned of enormous damage if US attacks resume. The Houthis blockaded Bab el-Mandeb to Saudi-flagged tankers; a Houthi strike on Najran injured six, two Saudi pilots died in a helicopter crash, Riyadh residents received a hostile aerial threat alert and the Kingdom Centre told tenants to work remotely; Saudi strikes in Yemen killed at least nine. Egypt said it would defend Saudi Arabia if asked. Trump endorsed Netanyahu ahead of Israel's 27 Oct election.","src":"CBS News live updates","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Araghchi sets Hormuz reopening on day six of a seven-day deal as UKMTO counts a wartime weekly high of 10 attacks","url":"https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/iran-war-us-trump-strait-of-hormuz-yemen-houthis-saudi-arabia/"},{"date":"2026-10-17","impact":"An order signed on 17 Oct waived every Russia-related operative section of the Graham Act, including tariffs of up to 500% on Russian oil and gas and up to 100% on the top buyers of Russian crude, a day before they were to take effect, and waived the provision keeping existing sanctions in place. OFAC had issued a general license through April authorizing Russian diesel imports, under which Russia would supply hundreds of thousands of tons of diesel to US and global markets; it is the second waiver in two weeks as the administration turns to Moscow to ease diesel prices. McConnell, Tillis, Collins and Cornyn urged Trump to rescind the deal; Schumer called it life support for the Russian economy; Zelenskyy said it helps only Russia. The White House said enforcing the sanctions now would impede peace negotiations. Lawmakers have introduced a bill to block the fuel purchases.","src":"ABC News","tag":"Trade","title":"Trump waives the Graham Act's Russia sanctions to keep a Russian diesel deal alive; four GOP senators object","url":"https://abcnews.com/Politics/trump-signs-order-waiving-graham-act-sanctions-russia/story?id=137341529"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"The S&P 500 fell 84.62 points to the week's low, ending a 55-day run without a 1% drop, and lost 1.9% on the week, its worst since June; the Nasdaq fell 572.99 points to 26,140.03, its biggest drop since July and down 4.5% on the week; the Russell 2000 fell 1.7% to 2,721.37; the Dow fell 0.3% to 52,002.41 but rose 0.7% on the week. The Philadelphia semiconductor index fell 3.8% with all 30 members lower, an eighth straight loss matching its longest streak since 2006; Micron fell 5.6%, Intel 5.8%, AMD 6.0%, Seagate 7.5%, Western Digital 6.9% and SanDisk 6.9% on Chinese memory competition and doubts about debt-funded AI capex. CoreWeave fell 11.9% to $61.39, down 25% on the week, with its 2031 notes above 16% and five-year CDS at 1,117 bp; Anthropic's planned November IPO reportedly faces resistance at a $2T valuation. Technology fell 3%; materials, utilities and energy rose. The VIX rose 21% to 19.99 after touching 22.31. The 10-year closed at 5.09% and the 2-year at 4.74%, Treasuries' best week since February. Gold settled at $4,181.10. The Fear and Greed index fell to 30 and AAII bears rose to 41.6%.","src":"AP via myMotherLode; Investrade; TradingKey; Investing.com; US Treasury","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 falls 1.1% to 7,659.44 and the Nasdaq 2.1% as the chip rout spreads; VIX closes at 19.99","url":"https://mymotherlode.com/news/national/11228202/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-friday-10-16-2026.html"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"Industrial production rose 0.7% after a 0.1% rise in August, revised from 0.0%, and is 2.2% above a year ago; the index rose at a 2.8% annual rate in Q3. Manufacturing rose 0.3%, utilities 3.5%, mining 0.2%; motor vehicles fell 3.0%, business equipment was flat and consumer goods rose 0.7%. Capacity utilization rose to 76.9% against 76.5% expected. Import prices rose 1.1% against 0.8% expected and 0.5% in August, and are 8.1% higher than a year ago; export prices rose 2.2% against 1.0% and are 11.6% higher. GDPNow stayed at 3.6% pending the 20 Oct update.","src":"Federal Reserve G.17; Investrade","tag":"Data","title":"Industrial production rises 0.7% in September against 0.4% expected; import prices up 1.1% and 8.1% on the year","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g17/current/default.htm"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"December Brent settled at $109.86 and November WTI at $101.41, up 3.5%, its highest close since 17 Sep; diesel settled at $4.90 a gallon. UKMTO reported 10 attacks in the week to 16 Oct, the busiest month since March, one ship sunk, and said most ships no longer report attacks. Iran's navy found a tanker on fire in the Gulf of Oman; an army spokesman said the strait reopens if the US leaves the region. CBS and CNN reported the Pentagon has prepared plans for a GBU-43/B strike on Iranian sites and Trump declined to rule one out after the 3 Nov election; Pezeshkian said in Yerevan that Iran is not seeking nuclear weapons and is ready for dialogue. Eurasia raised its escalation odds to 65% from 35%. The Saudi-led coalition intercepted a ballistic missile aimed at Yanbu, two cruise missiles and three drones; two missiles hit Aden airport; Houthi attacks have killed about 20 in Saudi Arabia. Iraq said the war has cost it $60B. Hormuz crude volumes topped 100M barrels last week, the US offered up to 17M SPR barrels, Ukraine struck the Ryazan refinery and lawmakers moved to block the Russian diesel deal.","src":"Bloomberg via Rigzone; CBS News","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Brent settles 3.7% higher at $109.86, its highest since May, as Hormuz attacks rise and Trump keeps a MOAB strike on the table","url":"https://www.rigzone.com/news/wire/brent_oil_hits_110_as_supply_risks_mount-16-oct-2026-184870-article/"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"Travelers reported core EPS of $9.18 against $7.43 expected on net premiums written of $12.41B, with catastrophe losses of $538M. Truist agreed to sell $7.7B of LightStream loans for $7.2B, guided Q4 net interest income to $3.65B to $3.7B and plans about $1B of Q4 buybacks. Regions earned $0.73 adjusted against $0.66 and raised 2026 net interest income guidance; M&T beat on EPS at $5.08 but missed on net interest income; Citizens earned $1.41 and raised its dividend 13%; Interactive Brokers missed by a cent with accounts up 35%. Netflix fell after M Science cut its Q3 subscriber-add estimate to 3.80M from 5.10M; it reports 20 Oct. J.B. Hunt missed at $1.82. Proofpoint agreed to buy Varonis for $6.3B.","src":"Investrade","tag":"Earnings","title":"Travelers beats with core EPS of $9.18; Truist, Regions, M&T and Citizens report; Netflix slips on a subscriber cut","url":"https://investrade.com/market-review-october-16-2026/"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"In a fireside chat with the IMF's Georgieva on 16 Oct, the last Fed remarks before the blackout, Warsh said he does not pre-judge meetings, that the Fed removed a dose of accommodation a month ago and will deliver price stability, that the world has broadly conformed to the Fed's expectations since September, that central bankers in Bangkok all hear the same thing about the surge in prices, and that AI is the most consequential change in his lifetime but the transition is likely to be very bumpy. The Fed will hold a field hearing on AI and productivity on 16 Nov in the Bay Area. CME pricing for a 28 Oct hike fell to about 15% from 28% before he spoke and from a 50% to 54% peak after the CPI; a December hike is priced near 89%, up from 83% a week earlier. Polymarket has October at 14% and December at 79%. The dollar index fell 0.3% to 101.79.","src":"Axios; Benzinga; Invezz; FXStreet","tag":"Policy","title":"Warsh in Bangkok: no pre-judging the October and December meetings; October hike odds fall to about 15%","url":"https://www.axios.com/2026/10/16/warsh-federal-reserve-interest-rates"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"PPI final demand rose 1.3% against 0.5% expected, the largest monthly rise since March 2022, and 6.3% on the year, the largest since December 2022. Energy rose 10.8% and gasoline 20.3%; final demand goods rose 2.6%, a series record; services rose 0.8%; core ex food, energy and trade rose 0.5% to 5.0% on the year. Retail sales rose 0.8% against 0.3% expected to $745.0B, with August revised up to 1.3%, but gasoline stations rose 6.4% and ex-autos-and-gas sales rose 0.2%; the control group rose 0.1% against 0.3%. Initial claims were 199K in the week to 10 Oct, a fifth week under 200K, with continuing claims at 1.710M. Empire State jumped to 25.5 from 7.6 against 10.3 expected; the Philadelphia Fed index eased to 20.9 from 37.8 with prices paid at 43.2. The 2027 Social Security COLA was set at 3.6%. GDPNow held at 3.6%.","src":"Bureau of Labor Statistics; Census Bureau; Department of Labor; Philadelphia Fed; investingLive; Babypips","tag":"Data","title":"September PPI 1.3% on the month and 6.3% on the year; retail sales 0.8% on gasoline; claims 199K","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/ppi.nr0.htm"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"The 10-year fell 9 bp to 5.10%, its biggest drop since August 2025, and the 30-year 9 bp to 5.43%, a fifth straight fall; the 2-year fell 5 bp to 4.77%. BofA's derivatives desk attributed the bid to AI-slowdown hedging and short covering. CME pricing for a 28 Oct hike fell to about 27% from 33%. The S&P 500 rose 25.12 points to 7,744.06, the Dow 1.1% to 52,177.69, the Russell 2000 1.4% and the equal-weight S&P 1.4%; the Nasdaq added 0.1% after being down 1.4% early and the SOXX fell 0.9% for an eighth straight day. Nike rose 5.2%, Equifax 7.3%, CoreWeave 6% after Jensen Huang's support with its CDS implying 60% five-year default odds; Nvidia fell 1.8%, TSMC 1.7% despite a record profit, Schwab 2.7% despite a 23% profit rise, Dell 6.8% and HPE 7.1%. The VIX closed at 16.46 after touching 18.01; the dollar index fell 0.5% to 102.06; gold rose 0.5% to about $4,192.","src":"AP via KRMG; TheStreet; US Treasury; Investing.com","tag":"Markets","title":"Yields fall through the hot PPI: 10-year 5.10%, S&P 500 up 0.3% to 7,744.06 on a broad rotation","url":"https://krmg.com/2026/10/15/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-thursday-10-15-2026/"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"December Brent fell 0.1% to $105.92 and November WTI rose 3% to $97.97, its sixth straight gain, after EIA reported an 8.6M barrel crude draw, the largest since February. CENTCOM confirmed an Iranian projectile sank the container ship GFS Pride and said more than 40 vessels transited Hormuz in 24 hours with the main routes clear of mines; Iranian media claimed a tanker on fire in the eastern strait. Trump said he would not use nuclear weapons against Iran; CNN reported plans for conventional MOAB strikes on energy and nuclear sites before or after the midterms, and Trump said the war would end quickly one way or the other and that oil would then fall. Reuters reported the administration is studying easing sanctions on shadow-fleet tankers. The Saudi-led coalition counted 20 Houthi attacks on Riyadh's airport this month, with 17 dead; Aramco offered extra spot cargoes to Europe; France began deploying assets to protect Saudi oil sites; the IEA said 330M barrels have been released with 100M to come; the EU-Gulf summit set for 24 Oct in Riyadh was postponed. Treasury sanctioned 16 individuals and 10 entities tied to Iran.","src":"Bloomberg via Rigzone; Fox News","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Brent settles at $105.92 as WTI jumps 3% on an 8.6M barrel draw; Trump rules out nuclear strike but strike plans reported","url":"https://www.rigzone.com/news/wire/oil_prices_diverge_as_supply_risks_mount-15-oct-2026-184860-article/"},{"date":"2026-10-14","impact":"Headline rose 0.6% on the month and 3.7% on the year, against 3.6% expected and 3.4% in August. Core rose 0.3% against 0.2% expected and 2.6% on the year from 2.4%, with shelter up 0.4% and airfares and used cars adding to the upside. Energy rose 5.2% in the month and gasoline 9.1%; energy is up 20.4% and gasoline 34.2% on the year. Food was flat. The next CPI is 10 Nov.","src":"Bureau of Labor Statistics; TheStreet","tag":"Data","title":"September CPI 3.7% headline and 2.6% core, both above consensus","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.htm"},{"date":"2026-10-14","impact":"CME pricing for a 28 Oct hike rose to about 35% from 22% a day earlier, after touching 44% at midday; December pricing had a hold at 4%, one hike at 55% and two hikes at 40%. Daly told Reuters more hikes are more likely than not but there is time to decide and no need to act in October. Bowman, in Bangkok before the CPI, said the Fed need not rush after September's hike. The Beige Book, with data to 5 Oct, reported modest growth, employment little changed, three districts with robust price growth and eight with moderate, higher oil in every district and five districts citing AI-related hiring restraint. Cook's lawyer Abbe Lowell asked the White House to move the 5 Nov hearing to after the 8-9 Dec FOMC, citing biased committee members.","src":"AP; investingLive; Reuters via Investing.com; Federal Reserve; Quartz","tag":"Policy","title":"Daly and Bowman see no urgency for an October hike; Beige Book flags broad price pressure","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/beigebook202610-summary.htm"},{"date":"2026-10-14","impact":"The S&P 500 lost 86.51 points, its fifth loss in six sessions since the 6 Oct record, ending the longest run without a 1% drop in more than six years. The Nasdaq fell 1.9%, the Russell 2000 1.9% and the Dow 0.1%. CoreWeave fell 15.5% as its bonds hit distressed levels, Oracle 8.8%, GE Vernova 6.2%, AMD 5.7%, Nvidia 3.2% and Broadcom 3.1%. The 10-year rose from under 5.17% to above 5.32% after the CPI and closed at 5.19%; the 2-year closed at 4.82%. The VIX rose 10% to 16.85. Gold fell 1.5% to about $4,100 and the dollar index closed at 102.22 after an 18-month high of 102.95.","src":"AP via KRMG; Babypips; US Treasury; Investing.com","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 falls 1.1% to 7,718.94, its worst day since July; 10-year spikes to 5.32% then closes at 5.19%","url":"https://krmg.com/2026/10/14/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-wednesday-10-14-2026/"}],"fed":{"label":"Tightening","note":"First hike since 2023 on 16 Sept; the Fed is in blackout until the 28 Oct decision, CME pricing for a 28 Oct hike is about 15% and a December hike about 89% per FXStreet on 19 Oct, Kalshi has an 85.5% hold and Polymarket 84.5% to 85.5%, with PPI at 6.3%, CPI 3.7%, import prices up 8.1% on the year and Brent near $110."},"headline":"The S&P 500 rose 0.4% to 7,690.73 on 19 Oct and the Nasdaq 1.1% as semiconductors ended an eight-day losing streak and the VIX fell to 18.44, while the 10-year rose 6 bp to 5.15% and the 30-year to 5.48%, Brent settled at $109.55, NAHB builder sentiment fell to 29, its lowest since June 2012, and a 28 Oct hike stays priced at about 15% ahead of Netflix on 20 Oct and Tesla and Meta on 21 Oct.","marketClose":"Mon 19 Oct 2026","notes":["September industrial production on this tab is the G.17 release of 16 Oct: +0.7% on the month, 2.2% on the year, with August revised to +0.1%. The trend tabs carry year-on-year rates computed from the FRED INDPRO levels, 2.25% for September, with June to August corrected for the revisions; months before June could not be rechecked.","September CPI on this tab is the BLS release: 3.7% headline and 2.6% core. The trend tabs carry 3.74% and 2.56%, computed from the NSA indexes. Core CPI at 2.6% against core PCE at 3.0% remains an unusual gap. September PPI is from the BLS release of 15 Oct.","Treasury yields are the 19 Oct Treasury par curve: 2-year 4.77%, 10-year 5.15%, 30-year 5.48%, 5-year 4.94%. Cappnotes shows the same closes; Investrade quoted the 10-year at 5.14%, up 4.2 bp, and FXStreet 5.13%. The high-yield spread is 15 Oct, 323 bp, because FRED had not posted 16 or 19 Oct.","The VIX close of 18.44 on 19 Oct is from Cappnotes and the Rio Times, both showing a 7.75% fall; ad-hoc-news cited a Cboe reading of 18.07 at 4:47 pm ET, after the 4:15 pm close, and the Investing.com table had no 19 Oct row. FRED VIXCLS is posted to 15 Oct and shows 16.87 for 14 Oct where the AP reported 16.85.","The dollar index close of 101.99 on 19 Oct is from Cappnotes, up 0.15%; FXStreet quoted about 101.95 and the Rio Times 101.946. Investing.com's 19 Oct row showed 101.82 with a 0.11 point range and looks partial; its 16 Oct close is now 101.84 against the 101.79 shown at the last refresh. FRED's broad dollar index is posted to 16 Oct at 121.40, which the trend tabs carry.","Brent on this tab is the December futures settlement, $109.55 on 19 Oct from Bloomberg via Rigzone; Investrade shows the same. The spot price on the trend tabs is the 14 Oct FRED posting of $126.55, the latest available; dated Brent was reported near $117 on 19 Oct, so the next FRED postings should fall.","The 28 Oct hike probability of about 15% is FXStreet citing CME on 19 Oct; Cappnotes has Kalshi at an 85.5% hold and Polymarket 84.5%, the Rio Times has Polymarket at 85.5% hold and 14.5% hike, and Forbes put the hold near 80%. December at about 89% is FXStreet; CNBC via Cappnotes said about 90%.","The Fed balance sheet for 14 Oct is the FRED WALCL posting of $6,755,639M; the next H.4.1 is 22 Oct. GDPNow is the 15 Oct estimate; the Atlanta Fed's next update is 20 Oct. The S&P 500 close of 7,690.73 is from the AP, up 31.29; Investrade shows 7,690 up 31.37."],"posture":{"label":"Neutral","note":"The S&P 500 rose 0.4% to 7,690.73 on 19 Oct and the Nasdaq 1.1% as semiconductors ended an eight-day losing streak, the VIX fell to 18.44 from 19.99 and the high-yield spread eased 2 bp to 323 bp on 15 Oct, but the 10-year rose 6 bp to 5.15%, the 30-year to 5.48%, Brent settled at $109.55 and NYSE decliners outnumbered advancers 1,761 to 945, so the call stays Neutral with the VIX 20 and spread 350 bp triggers still close."},"regime":{"label":"Expansion","note":"No framework indicator printed since 16 Oct: industrial production rose 0.7% in September and is 2.2% above a year ago, both ISM surveys sit near 55, GDPNow is 3.6% for Q3 and claims are 199K, while payrolls were 29K, consumer sentiment is 46.3 and NAHB builder sentiment fell to 29 on 19 Oct, its lowest since June 2012."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"−0.1","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":48.8},{"l":"Nov 25","v":48},{"l":"Dec 25","v":47.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":52.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":52.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52.7},{"l":"May 26","v":54},{"l":"Jun 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":55.6},{"l":"Aug 26","v":54.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.5}],"latest":"54.5","name":"ISM Manufacturing PMI","next":"2 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"54.6","read":"Above 50 for a ninth month but down two months running from July's 55.6. Prices paid jumped to 77.9 from 71.1, so the survey is flagging cost pressure more than demand weakness.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion","src":"ISM via First Trust","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://www.ftportfolios.com/Commentary/EconomicResearch/2026/10/1/the-ism-manufacturing-index-declined-to-54.5-in-september"},{"change":"+1.6","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":49.4},{"l":"Nov 25","v":47.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":47.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":57.1},{"l":"Feb 26","v":55.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":54.1},{"l":"May 26","v":56.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":56},{"l":"Jul 26","v":56.7},{"l":"Aug 26","v":53.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":55.3}],"latest":"55.3","name":"ISM Manufacturing New Orders","next":"2 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"53.7","read":"Our critical leading signal turned back up after August's drop. Production slipped to 56.7 from 58.3 and export orders fell to 50.9, so the rebound is domestic.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion","src":"ISM via First Trust","trend":"up","trendWord":"Accelerating","url":"https://www.ftportfolios.com/Commentary/EconomicResearch/2026/10/1/the-ism-manufacturing-index-declined-to-54.5-in-september"},{"change":"−0.5","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":52},{"l":"Nov 25","v":52.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":53.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":56.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":54},{"l":"Apr 26","v":53.6},{"l":"May 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":54},{"l":"Jul 26","v":54.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":55.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.9}],"latest":"54.9","name":"ISM Services PMI","next":"4 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"55.4","read":"A 27th month of expansion and just under the 55.0 consensus. New orders 59.8, employment back above 50 at 50.1, prices at 74.0, the highest since July 2022. No divergence from manufacturing: both sit near 55.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion","src":"ISM via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/united-states/non-manufacturing-pmi"},{"change":"−1.8","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":51},{"l":"Dec 25","v":52.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":56.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":56.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":49.8},{"l":"May 26","v":44.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":49.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":55.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":48.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":46.3}],"latest":"46.3","name":"UMich Consumer Sentiment","next":"23 Oct (Oct final)","period":"Oct 2026 prelim","prior":"48.1","read":"Preliminary October reading, below the 47.6 consensus. It is far below our 70 threshold and the lowest since the record low of 44.8 in May. Current conditions fell to a record low of 44.7 from 50.9, while expectations rose to 47.3 from 46.3. Year-ahead inflation expectations rose to 4.7% from 4.6% and long-run expectations to 3.5% from 3.4%.","ref":70,"refLabel":"70 = our weak/moderate line","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Weak, below 70","src":"University of Michigan via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Falling","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/united-states/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"−2.1%","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":1.444},{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.418},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.414},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.482},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.393},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.54},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.363},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.423},{"l":"May 26","v":1.41},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.374},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.433},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.403}],"latest":"1.403M","name":"Building Permits","next":"20 Oct","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"1.433M","read":"Revised up from a first print of 1.394M. Permits are 3.5% above a year ago but have gone sideways around 1.40M for three months, and single-family permits fell 1.6%. NAHB builder sentiment fell to 29 in October on 19 Oct, the lowest since June 2012, with 43% of builders cutting prices and mortgage rates well above 7%. September permits print on 20 Oct with a 1.39M consensus.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Flat trend","src":"Census Bureau via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slipping","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/united-states/building-permits/news/586545"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"+1K","hist":[{"l":"25 Jul","v":198},{"l":"1 Aug","v":200},{"l":"8 Aug","v":212},{"l":"15 Aug","v":207},{"l":"22 Aug","v":204},{"l":"29 Aug","v":207},{"l":"5 Sep","v":207},{"l":"12 Sep","v":198},{"l":"19 Sep","v":198},{"l":"26 Sep","v":199},{"l":"3 Oct","v":198},{"l":"10 Oct","v":199}],"latest":"199K","name":"Initial Jobless Claims","next":"22 Oct","period":"Week to 10 Oct","prior":"198K","read":"Under 200K for a fifth straight week and in line with the 200K consensus. The prior week was revised up 1K to 198K and the four-week average is 198.5K. Continuing claims fell 4K to 1.710M in the week to 3 Oct. Firms are not laying off, even with September payrolls at 29K.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Very low layoffs","src":"Department of Labor","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Steady","url":"https://www.dol.gov/ui/data.pdf"},{"change":"−104K","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-140},{"l":"Nov 25","v":41},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-17},{"l":"Jan 26","v":160},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-156},{"l":"Mar 26","v":214},{"l":"Apr 26","v":148},{"l":"May 26","v":63},{"l":"Jun 26","v":31},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-10},{"l":"Aug 26","v":133},{"l":"Sep 26","v":29}],"latest":"+29K","name":"Nonfarm Payrolls","next":"6 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+133K","read":"Well short of the 90K consensus, with July and August revised down by a combined 60K. The three-month average is 51K against our 200K to 300K healthy range. Government shed 17K jobs; manufacturing and construction added 20K.","ref":200,"refLabel":"200K = our healthy floor","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Below 200K benchmark","src":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Decelerating","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":4.4},{"l":"Nov 25","v":4.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":4.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":4.3},{"l":"May 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":4.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4.2}],"latest":"4.2%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"6 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"4.1%","read":"Mostly rounding: the unrounded rate was 4.175%. It has stayed between 4.1% and 4.3% since March, and participation rose to 61.8%. The Fed's September projection has it averaging 4.1% in Q4.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Stable, low range","src":"BLS via First Trust","trend":"up","trendWord":"Edging up","url":"https://www.ftportfolios.com/Commentary/EconomicResearch/2026/10/2/nonfarm-payrolls-increased-29,000-in-september"},{"change":"−0.4 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":0.64},{"l":"Oct 25","v":0.19},{"l":"Nov 25","v":0.47},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.24},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.37},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.25},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.13},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.47},{"l":"May 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.14},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.71},{"l":"Aug 26","v":0.25}],"latest":"+0.3%","name":"Durable Goods ex-Transportation","next":"27 Oct","period":"Aug 2026, m/m","prior":"+0.7%","read":"Still rising and up 11.2% on a year ago. Core capital goods orders, the business investment proxy, rose 1.6%. Monthly gains have slowed three months in a row, so watch whether September holds above zero.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"good","signalWord":"Capex cycle intact","src":"Census Bureau via First Trust","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower growth","url":"https://www.ftportfolios.com/Commentary/EconomicResearch/2026/9/25/new-orders-for-durable-goods-were-unchanged-in-august"},{"change":"+0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.44},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-0.18},{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.45},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-0.45},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.86},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-0.17},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.83},{"l":"May 26","v":0.06},{"l":"Jun 26","v":0.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.23},{"l":"Aug 26","v":0.06},{"l":"Sep 26","v":0.66}],"latest":"+0.7%","name":"Industrial Production","next":"17 Nov","period":"Sep 2026, m/m","prior":"+0.1%","read":"Rose 0.7% in September against 0.4% expected, with August revised up to 0.1% from 0.0%. Output is 2.2% above a year ago and the index rose at a 2.8% annual rate in Q3. Manufacturing rose 0.3%, utilities 3.5% and mining 0.2%, while motor vehicles fell 3.0% and business equipment was flat; capacity utilization rose to 76.9%. The hard data now matches the ISM survey, but the gain leans on utilities and nondurables rather than capex.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"good","signalWord":"Output catching up","src":"Federal Reserve G.17","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g17/current/default.htm"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"0.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":2.82},{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.72},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.87},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.94},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.96},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.11},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.08},{"l":"May 26","v":3.16},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3.04},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.98},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.01}],"latest":"3.0%","name":"Core PCE, y/y","next":"29 Oct","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"3.0%","read":"At or above 3.0% for six straight months. Headline PCE is 3.4%. Real spending rose 0.6% in August on a saving rate that fell to 4.1%.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = Fed target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above 2% target","src":"Bureau of Economic Analysis","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Sticky","url":"https://bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-august-2026"},{"change":"+0.3 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":3.02},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.7},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.65},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.39},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.43},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.29},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.78},{"l":"May 26","v":4.17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3.46},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.35},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.74}],"latest":"3.7%","name":"CPI, y/y","next":"10 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"3.4%","read":"Headline rose 0.6% on the month and 3.7% on the year, against 3.6% expected and 3.4% in August. Energy rose 5.2% in the month and gasoline 9.1%, and energy is up 20.4% on the year. Core rose 0.3% against 0.2% expected and is 2.6% on the year from 2.4%, with shelter up 0.4%, airfares and used cars adding to the upside. The pipeline is hotter: September PPI rose 1.3% on the month and 6.3% on the year on 15 Oct, with core PPI at 5.0%. The next CPI is 10 Nov.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = Fed target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above consensus, PPI 6.3%","src":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Accelerating","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.htm"},{"change":"+25 bp","latest":"3.75–4.00%","name":"Fed Funds Target","next":"28 Oct","period":"Since 17 Sep","prior":"3.50–3.75%","read":"Unanimous 12–0 hike on 16 Sept, the first since July 2023. The minutes show most officials judged another increase would likely be appropriate by year end. Chair Warsh, in Bangkok on 16 Oct and the last Fed voice before the blackout, said he does not pre-judge meetings and that the world has broadly conformed to the Fed's expectations since September. On 19 Oct CME pricing for a 28 Oct hike was about 15% and a December hike about 89% per FXStreet; Kalshi priced an 85.5% hold and Polymarket 84.5% to 85.5%. Forbes put the hold near 80%. UBS expects hikes in December and March 2027, then a pause. Daly said on 14 Oct there is time to decide; Williams, Collins and Barkin backed one more hike this year on 13 Oct. Governor Cook's lawyer asked to move the 5 Nov White House hearing to after the 8-9 Dec meeting. The blackout runs to the 28 Oct decision.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening bias","src":"Federal Reserve","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/monetary20260916a1.pdf"},{"change":"+$8.1B in a week","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.552},{"l":"Dec 25","v":6.641},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6.588},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6.614},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.657},{"l":"Apr 26","v":6.7},{"l":"May 26","v":6.704},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.736},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6.738},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.731},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6.743},{"l":"14 Oct","v":6.756}],"latest":"$6.76T","name":"Fed Balance Sheet","next":"22 Oct (weekly, Thursdays)","period":"14 Oct","prior":"$6.75T on 7 Oct","read":"Runoff has ended. Total assets were $6.756T on 14 Oct, up $8.1B from $6.748T on 7 Oct, per the H.4.1 posted to FRED on 15 Oct. The Desk has paused its reserve management purchases, so assets have drifted up near $6.75T after the $6.55T low in November 2025. Bank reserves averaged $3.03T in the week to 7 Oct. Liquidity is not the tightening channel this cycle. The policy rate and long yields are.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"QT over","src":"Federal Reserve H.4.1 via FRED","trend":"up","trendWord":"Drifting up","url":"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/WALCL"}],"sub":"Prices and the Federal Reserve's stance, read alongside the indicator set.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"+6 bp","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":4.02},{"l":"Dec 25","v":4.18},{"l":"Jan 26","v":4.26},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.97},{"l":"Mar 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":4.4},{"l":"May 26","v":4.45},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.44},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4.75},{"l":"Aug 26","v":4.75},{"l":"Sep 26","v":5.29},{"l":"19 Oct","v":5.15}],"latest":"5.15%","name":"10-Year Treasury Yield","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"5.09% on 16 Oct","read":"Closed at 5.15% on the Treasury par curve on 19 Oct, up 6 bp, after falling about 15 bp last week, its biggest weekly decline since February. The 30-year closed at 5.48%, up 5 bp, and the 5-year at 4.94%. Yields rose worldwide as European indexes fell more than 1% on weekend protests and Houthi attacks on Saudi facilities lifted oil. The closing high is 5.31% on 5 Oct and the intraday high 5.36% on 7 Oct, the highest since 2002; the year started at 4.16%. A $13B 20-year auction on 21 Oct, at a prior stop of 5.42%, is the next test, with the Fed in blackout until 28 Oct.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above 5%","src":"US Treasury","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound after best week since Feb","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_yield_curve&field_tdr_date_value_month=202610"},{"change":"+3 bp","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3.47},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.47},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.52},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.38},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.79},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.88},{"l":"May 26","v":3.98},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.14},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4.28},{"l":"Aug 26","v":4.34},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4.88},{"l":"19 Oct","v":4.77}],"latest":"4.77%","name":"2-Year Treasury Yield","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"4.74% on 16 Oct","read":"Closed at 4.77% on 19 Oct, up 3 bp, after three straight weekly declines. It is 11 bp below the 4.88% close on 30 Sep but 43 bp above end-August. It sits about 90 bp above the policy rate midpoint, consistent with roughly two more hikes: a 28 Oct hike is about 15% priced and December about 89%.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Prices more hikes","src":"US Treasury","trend":"up","trendWord":"Up on the day","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_yield_curve&field_tdr_date_value_month=202610"},{"change":"+3 bp","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":55},{"l":"Dec 25","v":71},{"l":"Jan 26","v":74},{"l":"Feb 26","v":59},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52},{"l":"May 26","v":47},{"l":"Jun 26","v":30},{"l":"Jul 26","v":47},{"l":"Aug 26","v":41},{"l":"Sep 26","v":41},{"l":"19 Oct","v":38}],"latest":"+38 bp","name":"2s10s Curve","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"+35 bp on 16 Oct","read":"Positive slope, so no inversion signal. The 19 Oct close is 10-year 5.15% less 2-year 4.77%, 3 bp steeper than 16 Oct because the long end rose more. It is 3 bp flatter than the 41 bp at end-September. The front end is pricing the Fed's patience into 28 Oct; the long end is pricing oil, supply and the 21 Oct 20-year auction.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = inversion line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Flatter than September","src":"FRED T10Y2Y; US Treasury par yields","trend":"up","trendWord":"Steeper","url":"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10Y2Y"},{"change":"+0.5%","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":6849.09},{"l":"Dec 25","v":6845.5},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6939.03},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6878.88},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6528.52},{"l":"Apr 26","v":7209.01},{"l":"May 26","v":7580.06},{"l":"Jun 26","v":7499.36},{"l":"Jul 26","v":7489.72},{"l":"Aug 26","v":7686.14},{"l":"Sep 26","v":7651.54},{"l":"19 Oct","v":7690.73}],"latest":"7,690.73","name":"S&P 500","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"7,651.54 on 30 Sep","read":"Rose 31.29 points, or 0.4%, to 7,690.73 on 19 Oct, recovering part of Friday's 1.1% fall; it is 1.6% below the 6 Oct record of 7,818.93, up 0.5% in October and 12.3% this year. The Nasdaq rose 1.1% to 26,430.89, the Russell 2000 0.3% to 2,728.30 and the Dow fell 0.1% to 51,966.03. The Philadelphia semiconductor index rose 1.6% to 11,316.34, ending an eight-session losing streak, its longest since 2010; consumer discretionary rose 1.8% and technology 1.4%, with Amazon up 5.0% on a Morgan Stanley top-pick call, while utilities fell 2.0%, real estate 1.2% and financials 0.8%. The rebound was narrow: the equal-weight index fell 0.3% and NYSE decliners beat advancers 1,761 to 945. Goldman upgraded CoreWeave to Buy with a $106 target, cut from $139; Cleveland-Cliffs rose 16.3% on a beat; Steel Dynamics fell 5.4% after hours on a miss. The KBW bank index has fallen six straight weeks, the longest since 2008. About 70 S&P 500 companies report this week, with LSEG putting Q3 earnings growth above 30%: Netflix on 20 Oct, Tesla, Meta, IBM, Texas Instruments and Lam Research on 21 Oct.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"1.6% off record","src":"AP via myMotherLode; Investrade; Cappnotes","trend":"up","trendWord":"Narrow rebound","url":"https://mymotherlode.com/news/national/11235328/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-monday-10-19-2026.html"},{"change":"+2.10","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":16.35},{"l":"Dec 25","v":14.95},{"l":"Jan 26","v":17.44},{"l":"Feb 26","v":19.86},{"l":"Mar 26","v":25.25},{"l":"Apr 26","v":16.89},{"l":"May 26","v":15.32},{"l":"Jun 26","v":16.45},{"l":"Jul 26","v":15.99},{"l":"Aug 26","v":14.92},{"l":"Sep 26","v":16.34},{"l":"19 Oct","v":18.44}],"latest":"18.44","name":"VIX","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"16.34 on 30 Sep","read":"Fell 7.8% to 18.44 on 19 Oct from 19.99, as the Nasdaq rose 1.1% and semiconductors bounced; the nine-day VIX fell 10.9% to 17.93. It is still 2.10 above the end-September close of 16.34 and above every month-end close since March. Friday's intraday high was 22.31. A close above 20 with the 10-year back above 5.31% is the volatility trigger in the conflicts list; the 10-year is 5.15%, so neither condition is met.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Back under 20","src":"Cappnotes; Rio Times; history from FRED","trend":"down","trendWord":"Down 7.8% on the day","url":"https://cappnotes.substack.com/p/closing-look-monday-october-19-2026"},{"change":"−2 bp","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":292},{"l":"Dec 25","v":281},{"l":"Jan 26","v":288},{"l":"Feb 26","v":312},{"l":"Mar 26","v":328},{"l":"Apr 26","v":283},{"l":"May 26","v":274},{"l":"Jun 26","v":275},{"l":"Jul 26","v":285},{"l":"Aug 26","v":263},{"l":"Sep 26","v":312},{"l":"15 Oct","v":323}],"latest":"323 bp","name":"High-Yield Credit Spread","period":"15 Oct","prior":"325 bp on 14 Oct","read":"FRED posted 323 bp for 15 Oct, the PPI day, 2 bp tighter than the 14 Oct reading of 325 bp and 1 bp below the 1 Oct peak of 324 bp; 16 and 19 Oct had not posted at this refresh, so Friday's chip selloff and Monday's bounce are not yet in the series. The spread is 60 bp wider than the end-August close of 263 bp. CoreWeave's bonds traded at distressed levels last week and Goldman's 19 Oct upgrade cited credit-market concerns, which is single-name stress that the index only partly reflects.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near October high","src":"ICE BofA via FRED","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slightly tighter","url":"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/BAMLH0A0HYM2"},{"change":"−0.3%","hist":[{"l":"7 Sep","v":97.17},{"l":"29 Sep","v":96.16},{"l":"6 Oct","v":100.58},{"l":"7 Oct","v":100.2},{"l":"8 Oct","v":104.28},{"l":"9 Oct","v":104.72},{"l":"12 Oct","v":106.65},{"l":"13 Oct","v":105.69},{"l":"14 Oct","v":106.06},{"l":"15 Oct","v":105.92},{"l":"16 Oct","v":109.86},{"l":"19 Oct","v":109.55}],"latest":"$109.55","name":"Brent Crude","period":"19 Oct settle","prior":"$109.86 on 16 Oct","read":"December Brent settled 0.3% lower at $109.55 on 19 Oct after touching $110 on Friday, its highest since May. November WTI, which expires 20 Oct, rose 2.2% to $103.66 while December WTI fell 0.5% to $98.54; dated Brent traded near $117, down almost $16 from a week earlier. Brent is up more than 75% this year and European diesel futures have more than doubled; Kpler says trend-following funds are at maximum long. Energy Secretary Wright said Gulf flows have recovered to about 80% of pre-conflict levels with refined products still constrained, and Iraq is exporting more than 3.4M barrels a day. Against that, UKMTO counted at least 10 vessels attacked in or near Hormuz in the week to 18 Oct, a weekly high since the war began, and 23 since 1 Oct; the IRGC said a supertanker hit a mine on 17 Oct, two tankers reported hits in the Gulf on 19 Oct, and Houthis targeted Yanbu. Iran said it has no choice but to assert control over the strait, while Araghchi said Hormuz would reopen on day six if the US meets Iran's seven-day conditions. Trump waived the Graham Act's Russia sanctions on 17 Oct to keep a Russian diesel deal alive. Sparta expects Brent to stay rangebound near $100.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Inflation input","src":"Bloomberg via Rigzone; Investrade","trend":"down","trendWord":"Near five-month high","url":"https://rigzone.com/news/wire/oil_prices_hover_near_fivemonth_high-19-oct-2026-184878-article"},{"change":"+0.15%","latest":"101.99","name":"US Dollar Index","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"101.84 on 16 Oct","read":"Rose 0.15% to 101.99 on 19 Oct as the 2-year rose 3 bp to 4.77%, after three daily falls from the 18-month high of 102.95 touched on 14 Oct. The euro closed at 1.1211 and USD/JPY at 158.49. The dollar is following US front-end yields; October hike pricing is about 15% and December about 89%.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Rate-driven","src":"Cappnotes; FXStreet; Investing.com","trend":"up","trendWord":"Steady","url":"https://cappnotes.substack.com/p/closing-look-monday-october-19-2026"},{"change":"0.0 pt","latest":"3.6%","name":"Atlanta Fed GDPNow, Q3","next":"20 Oct","period":"15 Oct estimate","prior":"3.6% on 8 Oct","read":"Unchanged at 3.6% on 15 Oct after the September retail sales and PPI data; the Atlanta Fed's next update is 20 Oct after housing starts. Industrial production rose at a 2.8% annual rate in Q3 per the G.17. The first official Q3 GDP estimate is due 29 Oct.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above-trend growth","src":"Atlanta Fed","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Steady","url":"https://www.atlantafed.org/cqer/research/gdpnow"}],"sub":"Closing levels for Monday 19 Oct unless a date is shown. The high-yield spread is the 15 Oct reading, the latest FRED has posted.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["A narrow rebound. The S&P 500 rose 31.29 points, or 0.4%, to 7,690.73, 1.6% below the 6 Oct record of 7,818.93 and up 12.3% this year; the Nasdaq rose 290.85 points, or 1.1%, to 26,430.89; the Russell 2000 rose 0.3% to 2,728.30; the Dow fell 36.38 points, or 0.1%, to 51,966.03. The Philadelphia semiconductor index rose 1.6% to 11,316.34, ending an eight-session losing streak, and Amazon rose 5.0% on a Morgan Stanley top-pick call, but the equal-weight S&P 500 fell 0.3% and NYSE decliners beat advancers 1,761 to 945. Utilities fell 2.0% and real estate 1.2% as yields rose. Goldman upgraded CoreWeave to Buy with the target cut to $106 from $139; the stock is down 43% since 11 Aug.","Yields gave back part of last week's fall. The 10-year closed at 5.15% on the Treasury par curve, up 6 bp, the 2-year at 4.77%, up 3 bp, and the 30-year at 5.48%, up 5 bp, so the curve steepened to 38 bp from 35 bp. Yields rose worldwide after European indexes fell more than 1% on weekend protests. A $13B 20-year auction on 21 Oct, at a prior stop of 5.42%, is the next supply test. The dollar index rose 0.15% to 101.99, the euro closed at 1.1211 and USD/JPY at 158.49.","Housing is the weakest hard-data point. The NAHB housing market index fell to 29 in October against 31 expected and 32 in September, the lowest since June 2012; current sales fell to 33 from 35, the six-month outlook to 32 from 37 and buyer traffic to 18 from 23. 43% of builders cut prices, the highest share since 2020, by an average 6%, and 65% used incentives, a 19th month at or above 60%, with mortgage rates well above 7%. Housing starts on 20 Oct have a 1.30M consensus after 1.275M and building permits 1.39M after 1.403M.","Oil held near a five-month high. December Brent settled 0.3% lower at $109.55 while November WTI, which expires 20 Oct, rose 2.2% to $103.66 and December WTI fell 0.5% to $98.54; dated Brent traded near $117, down almost $16 on the week. Energy Secretary Wright said Gulf flows are back to about 80% of pre-conflict levels and Iraq is exporting more than 3.4M barrels a day, but UKMTO counted at least 10 vessels attacked in the week to 18 Oct, a wartime weekly high, and 23 since 1 Oct; two tankers reported hits in the Gulf on 19 Oct and Houthis targeted Yanbu. Araghchi said on 18 Oct that Hormuz would reopen on day six if the US meets Iran's seven-day conditions. Trump waived the Graham Act's Russia sanctions on 17 Oct to keep a Russian diesel deal alive, drawing objections from McConnell, Tillis, Collins and Cornyn.","Volatility and credit stepped back from the triggers. The VIX fell 7.8% to 18.44 from 19.99, the high-yield spread tightened 2 bp to 323 bp on 15 Oct per the latest FRED posting, 1 bp below the 1 Oct peak, and the KBW bank index has fallen six straight weeks, its longest run since 2008. A 28 Oct hike is about 15% priced and December about 89%; the Fed is in blackout. GDPNow was 3.6% on 15 Oct and updates on 20 Oct after housing starts. About 70 S&P 500 companies report this week, with LSEG putting Q3 earnings growth above 30%."],"translation":[{"body":"The index rose 0.4% to 7,690.73 on 19 Oct after its worst week since June, and sits 1.6% below the 6 Oct record. The rebound was narrow: the Nasdaq rose 1.1% and semiconductors 1.6%, ending an eight-day losing streak, but the equal-weight index fell 0.3%, NYSE decliners beat advancers 1,761 to 945, and rate-sensitive utilities and real estate fell 2.0% and 1.2% as the 10-year rose 6 bp. Under our framework the supportive signals are PMI new orders at 55.3, claims under 200K, industrial production up 0.7% and GDPNow at 3.6%. The opposing ones are sentiment at 46.3, far below 70, payrolls at 29K, NAHB at 29, import prices up 8.1% on the year squeezing margins, Brent near $110, and a market whose leadership depends on debt-funded AI capex at a 10-year above 5%. Earnings growth above 30% is the cushion; Netflix on 20 Oct and Tesla, Meta, Texas Instruments and Lam Research on 21 Oct are the first big-tech and chip tests.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"Yields rose on 19 Oct after Treasuries' best week since February. The 10-year closed at 5.15%, up 6 bp, the 2-year at 4.77%, up 3 bp, and the 30-year at 5.48%, up 5 bp, so the curve steepened to 38 bp from 35 bp. The front end is pricing Warsh's patience: a 28 Oct hike is about 15% priced and December about 89%. The long end is pricing oil, global yields and supply: a $13B 20-year auction on 21 Oct at a prior stop of 5.42% is the next test, and the 10-year is 16 bp under its 5 Oct closing high. High-yield spreads tightened 2 bp to 323 bp on 15 Oct, 1 bp below the 1 Oct peak, and have not yet marked Friday's chip selloff; CoreWeave's distressed bonds remain the stress point for the AI-capex funding chain, and the KBW bank index has fallen six straight weeks.","title":"Fixed income"},{"body":"The Fed's balance sheet was $6.756T on 14 Oct, up $8.1B in a week, so tightening is still coming through the policy rate and long yields, not liquidity. The Fed is in blackout until 28 Oct; Warsh's last remarks on 16 Oct declined to pre-judge the October and December meetings, and on 19 Oct CME pricing had a 28 Oct hike at about 15% and December at about 89%, with Kalshi at an 85.5% hold. The data argues for more tightening: PPI 6.3%, CPI 3.7%, import prices up 8.1%, Brent at $109.55. Housing is where the tightening already bites: NAHB fell to 29, the lowest since June 2012, with 43% of builders cutting prices and mortgage rates well above 7%. Fiscal and trade policy is loosening energy constraints instead: Trump waived the Graham Act's Russia sanctions on 17 Oct to keep Russian diesel flowing, and the administration is letting sanctioned tankers legitimize cargoes. Governor Cook's lawyer asked to move the 5 Nov White House hearing past the 8-9 Dec meeting, so the independence question runs through the December decision.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
